// Blackjack (casino banking game, American-style hole card, no peek, no
// insurance) — stress-test DSL implementation. NOT part of the canonical
// corpus (see stress-test/README.md); written to test whether the language
// generalizes past its ten kernel-clean example games.
//
// Source: https://www.pagat.com/banking/blackjack.html (Pagat, this project's
// authoritative rules reference).
//
// Three punters (seats 0-2) against a fixed banker seat (seat 3, named
// `house` — deliberately NOT the stdlib rotating `dealer` slot documented in
// library.md's "Stdlib state": the casino banker never rotates, so reusing
// that name would misrepresent it). Each punter is dealt two face-up cards;
// the house gets one face-up card and one face-down hole card. Punters act in
// turn (hit/stand) starting to the house's left; the house then reveals its
// hole card and draws by fixed rule (hit below 17, stand at 17+) with no
// discretion at all — expressed with the SAME `round offering [...] ...`
// kernel Seven-Card Stud's betting uses, just with a single vocabulary word
// that carries no `when:` guard, so every turn the kernel offers the house
// has exactly one legal action. That is the answer to this stress test's
// first question: the language does NOT need a new construct for a
// rule-bound actor — the existing auction/betting `round` form already
// expresses "forced move" as a singleton legal-action set.
//
// Hand value uses the standard ace-high-then-reduce algorithm (`pip_value` /
// `hand_total` below): every card scores its ace-high pip value, and aces are
// downgraded from 11 to 1 one at a time — bounded by a single 52-card pack's
// maximum of 4 aces — until the total is 21 or under, or the hand is a
// genuine bust. That answers this stress test's second question: best-hand
// value with ace flexibility is expressible as a pure, non-recursive `elif`
// cascade, no loop required, because the physical deck bounds the ace count.
//
// Simplifications (recorded per CLAUDE.md and the stress-test brief):
//   - Fixed bet of 2 chips per punter per round, not literally "1" as the
//     brief suggested as an example. Rationale at `function payout`'s GAP
//     note: the language has no division operator at all (confirmed against
//     the grammar — `sum`/`term`/`factor` support only + - *), so a 1-chip
//     bet's exact 3:2 blackjack payout (1.5 chips) is inexpressible without
//     either a division op the language doesn't have or a rounding fudge that
//     would silently change the payout ratio the rules specify. A 2-chip bet
//     makes 3:2 land on an exact integer (3) with only integer arithmetic, so
//     it is the least-distorting fix — the ratio is exact, only the nominal
//     unit changed.
//   - No splits, no double-down, no insurance, no surrender. Splits/insurance
//     are explicitly out of scope per the brief; double-down and surrender
//     are cut for the same reason (each adds a variable-shape side hand or a
//     partial-forfeit payout this stress test doesn't need to reach the two
//     target stress points).
//   - No early dealer peek for blackjack: the house's hole card is dealt face
//     down at the start of the hand (line ~118) exactly as the American
//     convention has it, but is not inspected or revealed until every punter
//     has finished acting, so a punter can bust against a concealed dealer
//     blackjack and never gets to react to it. (This is NOT the "no hole
//     card" / European convention, where the dealer's second card isn't even
//     dealt until after the players act — a hole card genuinely exists here,
//     it is just not peeked at. Calling out the distinction because the
//     whole point of this stress-test corner is the hidden hole card.)
//   - No insurance (out of scope per the brief). Not a consequence of "no
//     peek" above — insurance is offered off the house's visible up-card, so
//     it would be just as expressible with peeking skipped; it is cut simply
//     because the brief scopes it out.
//   - Fixed 20-round session ("play a fixed number of rounds" per the
//     brief), single 52-card pack reshuffled every round (no multi-deck shoe,
//     no penetration/cut-card). `winner: highest stack` ranks all four seats,
//     so the house itself can be reported as the session's "winner" — an
//     honest side effect of forcing a terminal ranking onto a banking game
//     with no natural winner (Seven-Card Stud's cash-game terminal has the
//     same shape), not a distortion.

game Blackjack {

  players: 4
  direction: clockwise

  cards: standard52
  // No `ranking:`/`trump:` declared: both are optional (Big Two, Coup, French
  // Tarot, and Tichu all omit `ranking:` too), and Blackjack has no
  // trick-taking or rank comparison for either to serve — hand value is its
  // own arithmetic (`pip_value`/`hand_total` below), unrelated to `rank_value`
  // or `card_value`, which read the `ranking:`/deck-values table this game has
  // no use for.

  zones {
    deck         : Deck
    hand[player] : PublicHand<player>   // face-up cards: a punter's whole
                                        // hand; the house's up-card, then
                                        // (after reveal) everything
    hole[player] : Hand<player>         // only the house's slot is ever used:
                                        // the face-down hole card, hidden
                                        // from punters until reveal
  }

  state {
    house         : Player  = 3   // the fixed banker seat; never rotates
    stack[player] : Integer = 100
    round_num     : Integer = 0
    bet_amount    : Integer = 2   // fixed bet; see header for why 2, not 1
  }

  phase hand_sequence repeats until round_num >= 20 {
    before_each {
      move all cards to deck
      shuffle deck
      round_num := round_num + 1
    }

    phase play {
      state {
        in_hand[player]  : Boolean = false   // holds enough to cover the bet
        standing[player] : Boolean = false   // done acting this hand
        natural[player]  : Boolean = false   // dealt a two-card 21
      }

      for each player p: if is_punter(p) { in_hand[p] := stack[p] >= bet_amount }

      // Place the bets.
      for each player p: if is_punter(p) and in_hand[p] {
        stack[p]     := stack[p] - bet_amount
        stack[house] := stack[house] + bet_amount
      }

      // Deal: two face-up cards to every punter still in the hand; the house
      // gets one face-up card and one face-down hole card.
      for each player p: if is_punter(p) and in_hand[p] { deal 2 cards from deck to hand[p] }
      deal 1 card from deck to hand[house]
      deal 1 card from deck to hole[house]

      // A natural (two-card 21) settles without further action.
      for each player p: if is_punter(p) and in_hand[p] {
        natural[p] := hand_total(p) == 21
        if natural[p] { standing[p] := true }
      }
      natural[house] := hand_total(house) == 21

      // Punters act in turn, starting to the house's left; each keeps
      // getting offered a turn (hit or stand) until they stand or bust, then
      // action passes to the next punter still playing.
      round offering [hit, stand] from house offset_by left
            over players where still_playing(player)
            order priority
            until (number of players where still_playing(player)) == 0

      // Reveal the hole card: a movement from a hidden zone into a public
      // one, the same "flip" idiom Seven-Card Stud's showdown uses to turn a
      // hole card face up (move into hole[p], then out into the public zone).
      move all cards from hole[house] to hand[house]

      // The house is not a real decision-maker: `dealer_hit` is the only
      // vocabulary word offered and it carries no `when:` guard, so every
      // turn the kernel offers the house has exactly one legal action —
      // "forced play" on the same round-offering kernel Stud's betting uses,
      // not a new construct. Skipped entirely on a dealer natural (a
      // two-card 21 stands pat; there is nothing to draw to).
      if not natural[house] {
        round offering [dealer_hit] from house
              over players where player == house
              order priority
              until hand_total(house) >= 17
      }

      // Settle every punter still in the hand against the final house total.
      // The bet itself was already moved to the house's stack when placed, so
      // a payout of 0 correctly leaves it there (a loss); any positive payout
      // must come back out of the house's stack, or chips are created from
      // nothing (caught in testing: an unbalanced first draft left the total
      // chip count non-invariant across a round).
      for each player p: if is_punter(p) and in_hand[p] {
        stack[p]     := stack[p] + payout(p)
        stack[house] := stack[house] - payout(p)
      }
    }
  }

  winner: highest stack
}

move_type hit {
  effect {
    deal 1 card from deck to hand[actor]
    if hand_total(actor) > 21 { standing[actor] := true }
  }
}

move_type stand {
  effect { standing[actor] := true }
}

move_type dealer_hit {
  effect { deal 1 card from deck to hand[actor] }
}

function is_punter(p : Player) = p != house

function still_playing(p : Player) = is_punter(p) and in_hand[p] and not standing[p]

// Ace-high pip value (A always scores 11 here); `hand_total` below downgrades
// aces to 1 (subtracting 10 each) until the total fits or the hand is a
// genuine bust. Bare rank tokens are not enum values in this language — 2..10
// lex as integers, and there is no bare-name path for J/Q/K/A either — so the
// check compares against the string literal a `Card.rank` actually holds, the
// same idiom Pinochle's `has_marriage` and French Tarot's `is_pref_discard`
// use for exactly this reason.
function pip_value(c : Card) =
  if c.rank == "A" then 11
  elif c.rank == "K" then 10
  elif c.rank == "Q" then 10
  elif c.rank == "J" then 10
  elif c.rank == "10" then 10
  elif c.rank == "9" then 9
  elif c.rank == "8" then 8
  elif c.rank == "7" then 7
  elif c.rank == "6" then 6
  elif c.rank == "5" then 5
  elif c.rank == "4" then 4
  elif c.rank == "3" then 3
  else 2

function num_aces(p : Player) =
  (sum over hand[p] as c: if c.rank == "A" then 1 else 0) +
  (sum over hole[p] as c: if c.rank == "A" then 1 else 0)

function soft_total(p : Player) =
  (sum over hand[p] as c: pip_value(c)) +
  (sum over hole[p] as c: pip_value(c))

// Best hand value: the ace-high total if it is 21 or under; otherwise knock
// aces down from 11 to 1 one at a time until it fits or every ace has been
// downgraded (a genuine bust if still over 21 at that point). A single
// 52-card pack holds at most 4 aces, so a hand can never hold more than
// that — the cascade's depth is a fact about the deck, not an arbitrary cap.
// Function bodies are non-recursive pure expressions (no loop construct is
// available inside one), so this is written as a bounded `elif` chain rather
// than a "subtract 10 while over 21" loop.
function hand_total(p : Player) =
  if soft_total(p) <= 21 then soft_total(p)
  elif num_aces(p) >= 1 and soft_total(p) - 10 <= 21 then soft_total(p) - 10
  elif num_aces(p) >= 2 and soft_total(p) - 20 <= 21 then soft_total(p) - 20
  elif num_aces(p) >= 3 and soft_total(p) - 30 <= 21 then soft_total(p) - 30
  elif num_aces(p) >= 4 and soft_total(p) - 40 <= 21 then soft_total(p) - 40
  else soft_total(p) - 10 * num_aces(p)

// GAP: the real payout schedule is 1:1 (regular win) and 3:2 (blackjack).
// cardlang has no division operator anywhere in its expression grammar
// (`sum`/`term`/`factor` support only + - *), so `bet_amount * 3 / 2` cannot
// be written at all — not "written awkwardly," genuinely absent from the
// grammar. The `+ 3` below is only correct because `bet_amount` is pinned to
// 2 (winnings of exactly 3); it is not derived from `bet_amount` and would be
// silently wrong for any other bet size. See the header note for why 2 was
// chosen instead of the brief's suggested 1.
function payout(p : Player) =
  if hand_total(p) > 21 then 0                              // busted: forfeit
  elif natural[p] and natural[house] then bet_amount         // both naturals: push
  elif natural[p] then bet_amount + 3                         // blackjack pays 3:2
  elif natural[house] then 0                                  // dealer blackjack beats any other total
  elif hand_total(house) > 21 then bet_amount + bet_amount    // dealer busts: 1:1
  elif hand_total(p) > hand_total(house) then bet_amount + bet_amount   // higher total: 1:1
  elif hand_total(p) == hand_total(house) then bet_amount     // tie: push
  else 0                                                       // lower total: lose
